re things to go wrong—it's more ways to catch things that *did* go wrong. Think of it like a bank. You could keep all your money under your mattress (simple paper). But if your house burns down, you have no proof. A bank account (electronic) has multiple layers: a statement, a PIN, fraud monitoring, and transaction logs. Yes, those layers could theoretically fail, but they make it far *harder* for someone to steal your money without you being able to prove it. Elections are the same. I'm not "sure" e-voting works better in every case—bad implementation can ruin any system. But when done right, with public transparency, mandatory audits, and paper backups, it is demonstrably more reliable and more verifiable than paper alone. The real-world evidence from places like Colorado, where risk-limiting audits have been routine for years, backs that up: they've caught human errors in paper counts that would have gone unnoticed otherwise. Does that help clarify why I think the added layers are a net positive, not just extra complexity?
